const englishContent: PostContent = {
  title: 'What if we compared our timeline to the films of our childhood?',
  description: 'Talking houses, a smart car, air taxis, humanoid robots, brain-computer implants: the films of our childhood are meeting the present. A tour of the works that came true, and of what this timeline still costs.',
  html: `<div class="post-tldr"><p><strong>TL;DR</strong> The sci-fi we grew up on promised talking houses, helpful robots and voices we could hand our day to. Much of it has become real: video calls, talking cars, air taxis with certificates, <abbr title="Robots built to resemble the human body: torso, two arms and two legs.">humanoid robots</abbr> shipping in volume, <abbr title="Artificial intelligence">AI</abbr> voices that answer at conversation speed. We love living in this timeline, right down to the implants being tested in people with paralysis. Data from the last three months show fewer beginner hires in exposed occupations, and the cause is still open. That jobs question and dependence on a handful of providers remain real risks.</p></div>
<hr>
<figure class="post-cover"><img src="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/covers/martian-chronicles.webp" alt="First edition cover of The Martian Chronicles: Ray Bradbury's name and the title over a blue and orange sky crossed by orbits." width="300" height="437" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><em>The Martian Chronicles</em>, 1950, Ray Bradbury. First US edition cover (Doubleday), public domain according to Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption></figure>
<p>In 1950, <abbr title="American writer (1920-2012), author of The Martian Chronicles and Fahrenheit 451">Ray Bradbury</abbr> published a story about a house that talks. In the version that became a chapter of <em><abbr title="Science-fiction novel made of linked stories, published in 1950, about Americans settling Mars.">The Martian Chronicles</abbr></em> that same year, the house reads out the date: 4 August 2026.</p>
<p>That date is now two months behind us.</p>

<h2>A timeline someone already wrote</h2>
<picture class="section-loop loop-light"><source media="(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)" srcset="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/timeline-light.png"><img src="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/timeline-light.webp" alt="" width="240" height="120" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></picture>
<picture class="section-loop loop-dark"><source media="(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)" srcset="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/timeline-dark.png"><img src="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/timeline-dark.webp" alt="" width="240" height="120" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></picture>
<p>The sci-fi films and TV series we watched as kids had houses that talk, robots serving people and machines people speak to out loud. A surprising amount is already here.</p>
<p>Seven stories, and for each one, what we can check today. Some have a dark side, and we come back to it below.</p>
<p><strong>Bradbury's house.</strong> In "<abbr title="Short story by Ray Bradbury (1950): an automated house keeps running in a city destroyed by a nuclear bomb.">There Will Come Soft Rains</abbr>", the house announces the date at wake-up, cooks breakfast and recites a poem in the evening. The story is grim, but the house is a dream. Today a speaker gives the date and the weather, as <abbr title="Amazon's smart speaker, launched in November 2014 and run by the Alexa voice assistant.">Amazon's Echo</abbr> has since 2014. Bradbury's house has stepped out of fiction.</p>
<figure class="post-cover post-cover-left"><img src="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/covers/knight-rider.webp" alt="Knight Rider title card: the show's title above KITT, a black car, in a desert at sunset." width="320" height="180" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><em>Knight Rider</em>, 1982-1986, Glen A. Larson. Title card: ©&nbsp;rights holders, reproduced for illustration.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>The car in <em>Knight Rider</em>.</strong> The talking car of our childhood is <abbr title="Knight Industries Two Thousand, the intelligent car of the series.">KITT</abbr>. In the series created by <abbr title="American television producer, creator of Knight Rider.">Glen A. Larson</abbr> and aired on NBC from 1982 to 1986, Michael Knight, played by David Hasselhoff, fights crime alongside this artificially intelligent car, self-aware and nearly indestructible. KITT talked and drove itself. In 2026 both exist, in part. In a compatible Tesla you can talk to <abbr title="AI chatbot developed by xAI.">Grok</abbr>, which reached France and eight other European markets in February 2026: it answers questions and adds or edits navigation destinations. On the driving side, Tesla's <abbr title="Full Self-Driving (Supervised), Tesla's level 2 driver-assistance system: the driver keeps watching the road at all times.">FSD</abbr> is still supervised: the driver must watch the road and stays responsible. The cars with nobody at the wheel are <abbr title="Self-driving taxis booked through an app.">robotaxis</abbr>, in limited areas: <abbr title="Self-driving subsidiary of Alphabet, Google's parent company.">Waymo</abbr> in several US cities, Tesla in Texas and Florida, Baidu's <abbr title="Robotaxi service run by Baidu, a Chinese web search and AI company.">Apollo Go</abbr> and <abbr title="Self-driving company that runs robotaxis in China.">Pony.ai</abbr> in a few Chinese cities.</p>
<figure class="post-cover"><img src="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/covers/neuromancer.webp" alt="First edition cover of Neuromancer: the title and William Gibson's name in blue letters on a green grid, above a purple mechanical butterfly." width="245" height="406" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><em>Neuromancer</em>, 1984, William Gibson. First US edition cover (Ace): ©&nbsp;rights holders, reproduced for illustration.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong><abbr title="American-Canadian writer, born 1948, author of Neuromancer">William Gibson</abbr>'s hacker.</strong> In <em><abbr title="Science-fiction novel by William Gibson (1984), winner of the Hugo, Nebula and Philip K. Dick awards.">Neuromancer</abbr></em> (1984), a lone hacker breaks through a system's defences in <abbr title="Virtual space formed by computer networks. William Gibson popularised the word in Neuromancer.">cyberspace</abbr>, with an artificial intelligence on the crew. Machines now do that kind of work. In 2025, the final of the <abbr title="DARPA contest where teams build AI systems that find and fix security flaws in code.">AI Cyber Challenge</abbr> run by <abbr title="Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency, the US Department of Defense research agency">DARPA</abbr> had autonomous AI systems read more than 54 million lines of code. They found 54 of 63 planted flaws and patched 43, at about $152 per task, plus 18 real flaws nobody had known about.</p>
<figure class="post-cover post-cover-left"><img src="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/covers/back-to-the-future-2.webp" alt="Back to the Future Part II poster: Marty and Doc step out of the DeLorean, staring at their watches." width="259" height="384" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><em>Back to the Future Part II</em>, 1989, Robert Zemeckis. Poster: ©&nbsp;rights holders, reproduced for illustration.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="post-cover"><img src="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/covers/eh216s.webp" alt="A white EH216-S, cabin in the middle and arms carrying propellers, parked on a blue pad in front of the EHang stand." width="360" height="270" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><em>EHang EH216-S</em> at the Paris Air Show, Le Bourget, 20 June 2025. Photo: Artvill, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EHang_216-S_au_Salon_du_Bourget_2025.jpg">CC BY 4.0, Wikimedia Commons</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>The 2015 of <em>Back to the Future Part II</em>.</strong> Robert Zemeckis's 1989 film sends Marty McFly to 21 October 2015. It shows video calls, flat screens on the wall, tablets and screen glasses. All of that exists today, in the living room, in the pocket and on the nose. <abbr title="American screenwriter who co-wrote Back to the Future with Robert Zemeckis.">Bob Gale</abbr>, the screenwriter, has said the flying cars served the story. They ran late, and they are arriving: electric air taxis, or <abbr title="Electric vertical take-off and landing aircraft">eVTOLs</abbr>, exist. In China, <abbr title="Chinese company that builds pilotless electric aircraft.">EHang</abbr> says its <abbr title="EHang's pilotless electric aircraft, designed to carry passengers.">EH216-S</abbr>, a pilotless passenger aircraft, holds the civil aviation <abbr title="Type: approves the aircraft design. Production: approves the factory that builds it. Airworthiness: clears each aircraft to fly.">type, production and airworthiness certificates</abbr>. The company announced in March 2025 tickets for sightseeing flights in Guangzhou and Hefei, and the aircraft flew in public in Hong Kong on 28 August 2026. In the US, <abbr title="US maker of electric vertical take-off and landing aircraft.">Joby</abbr> reports five aircraft flying and twelve more in production. In the film, the future arrives through the sky. For us, it arrives through software too.</p>
<figure class="post-cover"><img src="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/covers/i-robot.webp" alt="I, Robot poster: Will Smith in a black coat, in front of a crowd of identical robots." width="260" height="385" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><em>I, Robot</em>, 2004, Alex Proyas. Poster: ©&nbsp;rights holders, reproduced for illustration.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="post-cover post-cover-left"><img src="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/covers/optimus.webp" alt="A white and black Tesla Optimus robot standing on a plinth in a showroom, between two cars." width="240" height="427" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><em>Tesla Optimus</em>, Tesla showroom, 18 November 2025. Photo: Sikander, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Optimus_bot_at_Tesla_showroom_-_20251118_-_01.jpg">CC BY-SA 4.0, Wikimedia Commons</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>The robots of <em>I, Robot</em>.</strong> In <abbr title="Australian film director of I, Robot, The Crow and Dark City.">Alex Proyas</abbr>'s 2004 film, starring Will Smith and loosely drawn from <abbr title="American science-fiction writer who created the Three Laws of Robotics.">Isaac Asimov</abbr>, humanoid robots bound by the <abbr title="Three rules imagined by Isaac Asimov, in priority order: protect humans, obey them, protect itself.">Three Laws of Robotics</abbr> fill the public services of Chicago in 2035. <abbr title="Virtual Interactive Kinetic Intelligence, the film's central AI.">VIKI</abbr>, the central AI, is its dark side. In April 2026, <abbr title="US company that builds humanoid robots.">Figure AI</abbr> said it had delivered more than 350 <abbr title="Humanoid robot model from Figure AI, the successor to Figure 02.">Figure 03</abbr> humanoids and was building one an hour. One of its robots loads and unloads a dishwasher. At Tesla, <abbr title="Humanoid robot developed by Tesla.">Optimus</abbr> is heading for the factory: on 22 April 2026, Elon Musk announced production at <abbr title="California city where Tesla runs a factory.">Fremont</abbr> this year, then a ramp-up in 2027.</p>
<figure class="post-cover"><img src="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/covers/unitree-g1.webp" alt="A grey Unitree G1 robot with a glowing blue face, standing on a red carpet at a trade show." width="240" height="405" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><em>Unitree G1</em>, Las Vegas trade show, November 2024. Photo: Sayanesy, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unitree_G1.jpg">CC0, Wikimedia Commons</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<p>China leads the market: according to <abbr title="Technology research firm.">Omdia</abbr>, Chinese companies made about 90% of humanoid shipments in 2025. <abbr title="Chinese company that builds legged robots, humanoids included.">Unitree</abbr> sells its <abbr title="Humanoid robot from Unitree, unveiled in May 2024.">G1</abbr> from $16,000 and puts it on stage: a boxing match streamed in May 2025 (remotely piloted by engineers, according to Popular Science), then the 2026 <abbr title="Television show broadcast every year in China for the Lunar New Year, since 1983.">Spring Festival Gala</abbr>, with <abbr title="Style of Chinese martial arts (zuiquan) that imitates the movements of a drunk person.">drunken-fist kung-fu</abbr>, <abbr title="Martial-arts weapon made of two sticks joined by a chain or cord.">nunchucks</abbr> and <abbr title="Discipline of getting past obstacles by running, jumping and climbing.">parkour</abbr> over tables. Official videos: <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.unitree.com/boxing">Unitree's page</a>.</p>
<figure class="post-cover post-cover-left"><img src="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/covers/her.webp" alt="Her poster: close-up of a man with a moustache, in a red shirt, on a pink background." width="220" height="314" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><em>Her</em>, 2013, Spike Jonze. Poster: ©&nbsp;rights holders, reproduced for illustration.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>The voice in <em>Her</em>.</strong> In <abbr title="American film director and screenwriter of Her.">Spike Jonze</abbr>'s 2013 film, a man who writes letters for other people for a living grows attached to Samantha, an operating system voiced by Scarlett Johansson. On 13 May 2024, <abbr title="US artificial intelligence company, maker of ChatGPT.">OpenAI</abbr> showed <abbr title="OpenAI model unveiled on 13 May 2024 that handles text, voice and images.">GPT-4o</abbr>'s voice and <abbr title="Co-founder of OpenAI.">Sam Altman</abbr> posted a single word: "her". The model answers speech in 320 milliseconds on average, about the pace of a human conversation.</p>
<figure class="post-cover"><img src="/visuals/nerd-in-the-ai-era/covers/upgrade.webp" alt="Upgrade poster: the face of a bearded man on a red background, the title UPGRADE in large white letters." width="220" height="326" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><em>Upgrade</em>, 2018, Leigh Whannell. Poster: ©&nbsp;rights holders, reproduced for illustration.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>The connected body in <em>Upgrade</em>.</strong> In <abbr title="Director and screenwriter of Upgrade.">Leigh Whannell</abbr>'s 2018 film, cars drive themselves in the near future, and a man who has become quadriplegic receives <abbr title="The film's implant, which lets its hero control his body.">STEM</abbr>, an implant that gives him back the use of his body. <abbr title="Devices that link brain activity to a computer.">Brain-computer interfaces</abbr> are being tested today in people with, among other conditions, a <abbr title="Damage to the spinal cord, which can take away the use of a person's limbs.">spinal cord injury</abbr>: in January 2026, <abbr title="A company that develops implantable brain-computer interfaces.">Neuralink</abbr> announced 21 participants in its trials worldwide. <abbr title="A company that develops brain-computer interfaces.">Synchron</abbr> tests its devices in North America and Australia, and <abbr title="A company co-founded by a former co-creator of Neuralink.">Precision Neuroscience</abbr> places its own on the surface of the brain. In China, <abbr title="A Shanghai company that develops brain-computer interfaces.">Neuracle</abbr>'s <abbr title="A coin-sized implant developed with Tsinghua University.">NEO</abbr> became, in March 2026, the world's first invasive brain implant approved for use outside clinical trials. These interfaces remain experimental.</p>
