<h2>Why we love it here</h2>
<p>We love being in this timeline. As kids, we watched these futures from the sofa. Now we run into pieces of them in the street, in the sky and in our pockets.</p>
<p>There is plenty of talk about <abbr title="A hypothetical AI that would outperform humans in nearly every field">superintelligence</abbr>, and the word has already made it into <abbr title="A directive signed by the US president that governs how federal agencies act.">an order</abbr>. On 29 September, the White House told US federal agencies to say “Super Intelligence” instead of “artificial intelligence”.</p>
<p>The scientific journal Nature sums up what researchers think: for most of them, superintelligence is a goal to reach, not a reality today. What already exists is enough to get excited about: capable AI that we talk to in plain sentences.</p>
<p>On the lab side, OpenAI announced on 8 September a solution to the <abbr title="Equations describing how fluids (air, water) flow. Proving that they always have a smooth solution in three dimensions, or showing the opposite, is one of the seven Millennium Prize Problems of the Clay Institute, worth one million dollars.">Navier-Stokes</abbr> problem, one of the seven <abbr title="Seven major mathematical problems that are still open, chosen in 2000 by the Clay Institute, with one million dollars for each. Only the Poincaré conjecture has been solved.">Millennium Prize Problems</abbr>. According to OpenAI, about 10,000 <abbr title="An AI program that writes, runs and fixes code from an instruction">agents</abbr> got there in 88 hours. For a Millennium Prize Problem to be recognized, the Clay Institute asks for a peer-reviewed publication, at least two years of scrutiny after that publication, and acceptance by the mathematical community. OpenAI's announcement is, for now, a company announcement awaiting that review. On 6 October, OpenAI published more results, with proofs that a computer checks in <abbr title="A programming language that lets a computer check a mathematical proof">Lean</abbr>. Each announcement then gets checked, and that is fascinating to follow.</p>
<blockquote><p>The films showed us the future. This timeline hands us the tools.</p></blockquote>
<p>That is the sense in which it was made for us: for people who build and like to try things.</p>

<h2>What this timeline asks of us</h2>
<p>It was not made for everyone. It assumes spare time, a job that leaves some energy for the evening, and enough grounding to judge what the AI hands back. Plenty of people do not have all three at once.</p>
<p>To my mind, though, it gives room and importance to many kinds of people: <abbr title="Said of people whose cognitive functioning differs from that of the majority. A broad term, not a diagnosis.">neurodivergent</abbr> people, enthusiasts, the obsessed, those who tinker non-stop and those who love to create. I believe it puts them in the spotlight, and that is one more reason to love it.</p>
<p>There is a dystopian reading of this timeline, and it holds up. Jobs are changing, and the worry about employment is legitimate. Here is what the publications from 11 July to 11 October 2026 show.</p>
<p>Beginners are first in line. According to the <abbr title="The French central bank, which also publishes economic research">Banque de France</abbr> (8 October), permanent-contract hires of 21-29 year-olds stood in March 2026 about 28% below their November 2022 level in the fifth of occupations most exposed to <abbr title="Artificial intelligence that produces text, code or images from an instruction">generative AI</abbr>, against 8% in the least exposed fifth. According to the <abbr title="A Stanford University lab that studies the digital economy">Stanford Digital Economy Lab</abbr> (12 August), US employment of 22-25 year-olds in exposed occupations is 19% below that of their less exposed peers, based on payroll data from <abbr title="Automatic Data Processing, a US payroll company">ADP</abbr>. In both studies, the gap runs through hiring.</p>
<p>For developers, the <abbr title="A Paris research institute for economics">Paris School of Economics</abbr> (7 September) finds in <abbr title="The French public employment service, formerly Pôle emploi">France Travail</abbr> postings that specialist <abbr title="Information technology: software, systems, networks">IT</abbr> occupations were about 30% lower in early 2025 than at the end of 2022, against occupations that need no IT skills. In those postings, the share asking for programming falls. The share asking for public speaking rises.</p>
