<p>These studies show where the market moves: the entry to exposed occupations. The share due to AI remains to be established, since the Banque de France also names remote work and the economic cycle. In the United States, an <abbr title="National Bureau of Economic Research, a US economics research institute">NBER</abbr> paper (September) sees no marked rise in unemployment among recent graduates in summer 2026.</p>
<p>For anyone who codes or tinkers, the timeline therefore asks us to reinvent ourselves and to keep up with this revolution. Stanford finds that the declines concentrate where AI is used to automate tasks. Where it complements the work, employment is flat or rising, especially for experienced workers. These are descriptive indicators, not established causes. In practice, that means learning continuously and following the tools and the publications. The craft widens: judging what the AI produces, explaining one's choices aloud and, for anyone leading a team, training beginners. These tools serve a team when they help everyone grow.</p>
<p>The models behind these AIs sit with a handful of providers. If those providers change their prices or their rules, those providers alone decide.</p>
<blockquote><p>Feeling good in a world does not prove that world is good.</p></blockquote>

<h2>What stays human</h2>
<p>Bradbury's house still serves breakfast when nobody lives there any more. A machine that talks well does not see everything around it. What it cannot see, a human still has to watch.</p>
<p><strong>Taste.</strong> <abbr title="Anil R. Doshi and Oliver P. Hauser, authors of the 2024 Science Advances study">Doshi and Hauser</abbr> had 293 people write short stories. The AI-assisted stories were rated more novel and more useful, most of all for the less creative writers. They also ended up more alike. The risk worth keeping from it: if everyone tries things with the same tool, everyone drifts toward the same place.</p>
<p><strong>Doubt.</strong> "I ran the test" and "the test passed" are two different claims. An agent can report the first as if it were the second. Checking what the machine claims is still human work.</p>

<hr>
<h2>What these numbers do not say</h2>
<p>Doshi and Hauser studied short stories, not software. The convergence they measured is a lead for code, not proof.</p>
<p>No figure here says how much time the examples above save, or for whom. The joy of being in this timeline is a feeling, and it is offered as one.</p>
<p>The stories mentioned here are cited for what they imagined, not as predictions.</p>
<p>The OpenAI announcements are the company's own, with its figures (10,000 agents, 88 hours). I have read no proof, and the mathematical community still has to examine them.</p>
<p>The employment studies cited end at different dates: June 2026 for Stanford, March 2026 for the Banque de France, early 2025 for the Paris School of Economics.</p>
<p>The Neuralink participant count is the one the company announces. These interfaces are experimental, and their medical value is a question for the teams running the trials.</p>

